## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of how CPU frequency scaling and turbo decisions influence latency. It also assesses ability to use default tools for correlation, triage threads, and apply targeted mitigations without disturbing others.

## Key Concepts

- CPU frequency scaling and turbo boost
- Per-core scheduling and affinity
- Observability: perf, tracepoints, kernel logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// diag.js
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
try {
  const g = execSync("grep -h 'scaling_governor' /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_governor", {stdio:['ignore','pipe','ignore']}).toString().trim();
  console.log("Governors:\n" + g);
} catch {
  console.log("Governors unavailable");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this workflow for NUMA or SMT?
- What changes if you cannot modify the governor on all CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical debugging of ephemeral ports and TIME_WAIT behavior under bursts, a common production pitfall for web services.

## Key Concepts
- Ephemeral port range, TIME_WAIT backlog, FD limits
- net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse and ip_local_port_range
- Observability: ss, /proc, ulimit, and service limits

## Code Example
```javascript
// Quick Node snippet to count TIME_WAIT entries from /proc/net/tcp
const fs = require('fs');
const lines = fs.readFileSync('/proc/net/tcp','utf8').split('\n').slice(1);
const count = lines.filter(l => l.trim().split(/\s+/)[3] === '06').length;
console.log(`TIME_WAIT sockets: ${count}`);
```
